DESCRIPTION:Join the School of Data and Information Sciences for the Kilgou
 r Lecture in Information Science\, featuring Hannah Cyrus\, digital media 
 librarian at Bangor Public Library. Cyrus will present the lecture\, “Bu
 ilding Solidarity with Library Patrons through Tech Refusal\,” on Wednes
 day\, Oct. 28\, from 3:30 to 4:15 p.m. in Carolina Union Room 3411 and via
  Zoom.\n\n \n\nA Q&A will take place from 4:15 to 4:30 p.m.\, followed by 
 a reception and refreshments from 4:30 to 5 p.m. Students can earn CLE cre
 dit for attending.\n\n \n\nThe event is open to the public. Register onlin
 e and indicate whether you plan to attend in person or online.\n\n \n\nAbs
 tract: \n\nLibrary patrons\, like library workers\, are increasingly expec
 ted to make use of digital technologies to work\, manage their daily needs
 \, and access information. Frequently\, these technologies are delivered b
 y some of the largest\, most powerful corporations in the world\, and the 
 privacy\, mental health and environmental impacts of using them often run 
 counter to the library profession’s stated values. As librarianship requ
 ires more and more understanding of digital products\, how can librarians\
 , as professionals\, connect with patrons\, working to build power togethe
 r in the face of the pressures to adopt different technologies?\n\n \n\nIn
  this lecture\, Hannah Cyrus\, Digital Media Librarian at the Bangor Publi
 c Library\, will share stories from her technology instruction work and ex
 plore how libraries are positioned to work with our communities on technol
 ogy questions. Library workers can build mutual understanding and prioriti
 ze patron autonomy through honoring skepticism towards technology\, combat
 ting assumptions and dominant narratives around tech\, and keeping refusal
  a viable option. At a time when government agencies are harnessing the ma
 ss surveillance infrastructure built by tech companies\, AI use is becomin
 g a prerequisite for professional advancement in many fields\, and using t
 he internet is feeling less fun than ever\, many people are re-thinking th
 eir relationships with technology – from returning to flip phones to cut
 ting down license plate reader cameras. As trusted sources of information\
 , library workers can and should be part of these conversations\, working 
 with our patrons to meet their needs while holding true to professional va
 lues.\n\n \n\nMore about Hannah Cyrus\n\nHannah Cyrus works as the digital
  media librarian at Bangor Public Library in Bangor\, Maine\, with a focus
  on technology instruction and digital privacy. Her work often centers aro
 und patrons concerned about their accounts being compromised\, overwhelmed
  with junk email and spam phone calls\, or concerned by the ubiquitous cre
 epiness of online surveillance capitalism. Hannah received her Master of S
 cience in Library & Information Science from Simmons University in 2016. S
 he is a member of the Library Freedom Project and a recipient of the 2026 
 Jim Campbell Intellectual Freedom Award from the Maine Library Association
 . Her article\, “Refusal as Instruction: Equipping Patrons to Resist AI\
 , Data Brokers\, Big Tech\, & More\,” was published in Information Techn
 ology & Libraries in March of 2026.\n\n \n\nAbout the OCLC/Frederick G. Ki
 lgour Lecture in Information Science\n\nThe Frederick G. Kilgour Lecture w
 as established in 2004 by the Online Computer Library Center (OCLC) on the
  occasion of Dr. Kilgour’s 90th birthday. Kilgour was a Distinguished Re
 search Professor Emeritus at the School of Information and Library Science
  (SILS) and Founder of OCLC. Of his many innovations\, he is most noted fo
 r inventing the WorldCat database in 1971. Today\, OCLC is a nonprofit glo
 bal library cooperative providing shared technology services\, original re
 search\, and community programs so that libraries can better fuel learning
 \, research and innovation.
LOCATION:Carolina Union\, Room 3411
SUMMARY:Kilgour Lecture featuring Hannah Cyrus
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/kilgour-lecture-featuring-hann
 ah-cyrus
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
