DTSTAMP:20261128T073142Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54246381462377
DTSTART:20261104T223000Z
DTEND:20261105T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:A talk by Dr. Ian Shin (Department of American Culture\, Univer
 sity of Michigan)\, co-sponsored with the Department of History. \nHow can
  we understand the relationship between art collecting and Western imperia
 lism as more than simply a story of victim and aggressor\, of the plundere
 d and the plunderer? From the Gilded Age to World War II\, elite collector
 s and museums in the United States transformed from owning a smattering of
  Chinese porcelains to possessing some of the world’s largest and most s
 ophisticated collections of Chinese art. \nIn this talk\, historian Ian Sh
 in shows that\, beyond aesthetic taste and economics\, geopolitics were vi
 tal to this transformation. Collecting and studying Chinese art honed Amer
 icans’ belief that they should dominate Asia and the Pacific Ocean throu
 gh the ideology of “imperial stewardship.” U.S. imperial stewardship e
 ncompassed both genuine curiosity and care for Chinese art\, and the endur
 ing structures of domination that underpinned the growing transpacific art
  market in the early twentieth century. Far more than a history of cultura
 l “exchange” between the United States and China\, this is also a hist
 ory of rivalries and feuds with European curators and enterprising Chinese
  merchants. \nUltimately\, this talk challenges us to come to terms with h
 ow our interest in and desire for beautiful art are inextricable from ques
 tions of power. \nFree\, but space is limited\; registration required belo
 w. \n  \nThis event is part of UNC–Chapel Hill’s Campus Life Experienc
 e (CLE) program. Students who attend can scan the QR code provided at the 
 event to receive CLE credit.
GEO:35.912593;-79.054867
LOCATION:Ackland Art Museum
SUMMARY:Porcelains and Power: The Politics of Chinese Art in the Age of Ame
 rican Ascendancy
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/porcelains-and-power-the-polit
 ics-of-chinese-art-in-the-age-of-american-ascendancy
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073142Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54410309090535
DTSTART:20261104T223000Z
DTEND:20261104T233000Z
DESCRIPTION:Please join former U.S. ambassador to Algeria Elizabeth Aubin f
 or a talk on “Algeria and European Energy Security: Supply\, Investment 
 and the U.S. Opportunity.” Aubin will explore disruptions over the last 
 five years in Europe’s energy supply and highlight Algeria’s strategic
  role as an underleveraged partner for European energy security.\n\n \n\nW
 ednesday\, Nov. 4\n5:30 p.m.\nRoom 1005\, FedEx Global Education Center\n\
 nPlan to attend? Let us know!\n\n \n\nAubin is visiting Chapel Hill under 
 the auspices of the Diplomacy Initiative’s new Visiting Diplomats series
 . Attendees will also have the chance to hear her describe her career as a
  management-track Foreign Service Officer on Thursday\, Nov. 5.\n\n \n\nLe
 arn more about Elizabeth Aubin\n\nElizabeth Moore Aubin is the former U.S.
  Ambassador for the People’s Republic of Algeria and architect for the l
 andmark security cooperation agreement signed between Algeria and the Unit
 ed States. She is the recipient of several awards honoring her service\, i
 ncluding the Charles E. Cobb Award for Initiative and Success in Trade Dev
 elopment and Commercial Diplomacy. Her distinguished career with the U.S. 
 Department of State has taken her around the world\, with postings includi
 ng Canada\, Belgium and Algeria. Aubin now serves as an Atlantic Council D
 istinguished Fellow\, board director with the World Affairs Council of Ham
 pton Roads\, and board member for the liaison committee to the USS Newport
  News. She is also a guest speaker/lecturer. Aubin has a bachelor’s degr
 ee in political science from Barnard College and speaks fluent French.
GEO:35.907828;-79.054177
LOCATION:FedEx Global Education Center\, Room 1009
SUMMARY:‘Algeria and European Energy Security: Supply\, Investment and th
 e US Opportunity’ with Elizabeth Aubin
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/algeria-and-european-energy-se
 curity-supply-investment-and-the-us-opportunity-with-elizabeth-aubin
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
