UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54302699165130
DTSTART:20261109T193000Z
DTEND:20261109T203000Z
DESCRIPTION:Chair of the Faculty Michael Gerhardt is hosting a panel on the
  First Amendment and how it protects free expression on our campus. All fa
 culty\, staff and students are invited to attend.\n\n \n\nSuzanne Globetti
  (moderator) is a teaching associate professor in the political science de
 partment at UNC. She teaches courses in American government\, including th
 e introductory course\, campaigns and elections\, and analyzing public opi
 nion.\n\n \n\nPrior to joining the UNC faculty\, she taught at Bowdoin Col
 lege and Vanderbilt University\, where she received the Harriet S. Gilliam
  Award for Excellence in Teaching and the Robert Birkby Teaching Award. Gl
 obetti received her Ph.D. from the University of Texas at Austin.\n\n \n\n
 Daniel Rice (panelist) is an assistant professor at UNC Law\, where he tea
 ches constitutional \, First Amendment\, and federal Indian law. His recen
 t scholarship explores the interaction between cultural and legal change\,
  the interpretive significance of moral outrage\, and the geopolitical fou
 ndations of federal Indian law. Rice’s work has appeared in several lead
 ing law reviews.\n\n \n\nPrior to joining academia\, Rice clerked for Judg
 e Sri Srinivasan of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the D.C. Circuit\, as we
 ll as Judge Christopher Cooper of the U.S. District Court for the District
  of Columbia. Rice also worked as an attorney at the Institute for Constit
 utional Advocacy and Protection at the Georgetown University Law Center. R
 ice graduated summa cum laude from the Duke University School of Law. He i
 s a citizen of the Cherokee Nation.\n\n \n\nMark Storslee is an associate 
 professor and C. Boyden Gray Distinguished Scholar at UNC Law. His scholar
 ship focuses on constitutional history\, religious freedom\, and First Ame
 ndment law generally.\n\n \n\nStorslee clerked for Judge Diarmuid O’Scan
 nlain on the United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\, and la
 ter for Associate Justice Neil Gorsuch on the United States Supreme Court.
  He also previously served as the Executive Director of the Constitutional
  Law Center at Stanford Law School. He holds a J.D. from Stanford Law Scho
 ol and a Ph.D. in Religious Studies (ethics) from the University of Virgin
 ia.
GEO:35.90984;-79.050769
LOCATION:Wilson Library \, Pleasants Family Assembly Room
SUMMARY:First Amendment Panel
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/first-amendment-panel
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073142Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54106610392901
DTSTART:20261109T223000Z
DTEND:20261110T000000Z
DESCRIPTION:Unmaking Eden: Rethinking Domestication\n\n \n\nDavid Carr\, Pr
 ofessor of Old Testament at Union Theological Seminary\, offers a new read
 ing of Genesis through the lens of human-animal studies. Drawing on his ne
 w book\, Unmaking Eden\, Carr asks how these ancient stories have shaped i
 deas about humans\, animals\, and our relationship to the natural world.
GEO:35.907852;-79.050272
LOCATION:The Sonja Haynes Stone Center for Research in Black Culture and Hi
 story\, Hitchcock room
SUMMARY:Heilig Lecture: David Carr
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/heilig-lecture-david-carr
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073142Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54007493240432
DTSTART:20261110T003000Z
DTEND:20261110T020000Z
DESCRIPTION:Jake Gyllenhaal delivers a career-defining performance as an am
 bitious freelancer who prowls Los Angeles after dark in search of footage 
 for local television news. The city’s nocturnal landscape of police sire
 ns\, flashing lights\, and breaking stories becomes the backdrop for a mor
 dant examination of ambition\, competition\, and media culture in the twen
 ty-first century. \nThis film will be introduced by Kino Korner\, graduate
  student association. \nIt will be screened at 7:30 p.m. at The Lumina The
 ater (620 Market St\, Chapel Hill\, NC 27516). Tickets are free for all au
 dience members\, and registration is required via The Lumina’s webpage. 
 When this link is live\, click to reserve your free ticket. \nUNC-Chapel H
 ill students can receive CLE credit for attending by scanning the QR code 
 at the event. \n  \nAbout the Series: \nJoin the Ackland Film Forum this f
 all for Socially Charged Crime Thrillers: Snapshots of Social Anxiety\, a 
 series that accompanies the Ackland exhibition Care and Concern: Recent Ac
 quisitions of Modern and Contemporary Art. \nAs vehicles of social comment
 ary and stylistic bravura\, crime thrillers often register the pressures a
 nd contradictions of their historical moment. Across nearly half a century
 \, the films in this series stage encounters with political power\, public
  trust\, inequality\, and ambition through assassination plots\, botched r
 obberies\, and media-driven spectacle. \nFrom the crises of the late 1960s
  to the intensified media environments of the twenty-first century\, these
  works show how genre cinema can capture social anxiety in real time. Each
  film stands both as a gripping narrative and as a record of a world under
  strain\, where institutions falter and everyday life becomes fraught with
  conflict. \nThe Ackland Film Forum is co-organized by the Ackland Art Mus
 eum and UNC Film Studies\, housed in the UNC-Chapel Hill Department of Eng
 lish and Comparative Literature.
LOCATION:The Lumina Theater\, 620 Market Street\, Chapel Hill\, 27516\, Uni
 ted States
SUMMARY:Ackland Film Forum: "Nightcrawler" (Dan Gilroy\, 2014)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ackland-film-forum-nightcrawle
 r-dan-gilroy-2014
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073142Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54187615213645
DTSTART:20261110T130000Z
DTEND:20261110T220000Z
