DESCRIPTION:Is AI a threat to human health and happiness\, or can it be har
 nessed to improve human wellbeing? How can we maximize its benefits while 
 minimizing its risks?\n\n \n\nJoin us to hear a range of perspectives on h
 ow AI may affect human wellbeing. Alessandra Bazzano is a professor who st
 udies the relationship between AI and public health. Tyler Cowen is an eco
 nomist and professor at George Mason University. Ben Levinstein is a philo
 sopher and member of technical staff at Anthropic. The conversation will b
 e moderated by UNC journalism professor Deb Aikat.\n\n \n\nThe event is co
 -sponsored by The Office of the Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost. \n\
 n \n\nStudents who attend in person can earn CLE credit. No tickets necess
 ary\; seating is first-come\, first-served. Reserved tickets are available
  for guests coming from outside Chapel Hill\; email publicdiscourse@unc.ed
 u to reserve seats. Join us for pizza in the lobby afterward!
LOCATION:Frank Porter Graham Student Union Auditorium
SUMMARY:AI and Human Wellbeing
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ai-human-and-wellbeing
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073142Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54436486357907
DTSTART:20261112T223000Z
DTEND:20261112T233000Z
DESCRIPTION:What can Patagonia's former CEO teach us about doing things dif
 ferently?\n\n \n\nFrom serving as a Navy SEAL to leading one of the world'
 s most recognizable outdoor brands\, Michael Crooke has built a career on 
 challenging convention\, leading with purpose\, and redefining what succes
 s looks like.\n\n \n\nJoin the Shuford Program in Entrepreneurship for our
  annual Shuford Lecture\, Business as Unusual\, featuring Michael Crooke\,
  former CEO of Patagonia\, on Thursday\, November 12\, at 5:30 PM at the V
 arsity Theatre in Chapel Hill.\n\n \n\nThrough an engaging fireside chat\,
  Crooke will share lessons from his unconventional career\, exploring how 
 values-driven leadership\, innovative thinking\, and the courage to challe
 nge the status quo can shape successful businesses and meaningful careers.
 \n\n \n\nWhether you're an aspiring entrepreneur\, a future leader\, or si
 mply figuring out your next big move\, this conversation will challenge yo
 u to rethink traditional definitions of success and discover how doing thi
 ngs differently can lead to extraordinary possibilities.\n\n \n\nCome curi
 ous. Leave inspired.
GEO:35.913856;-79.054978
LOCATION:Varsity Theater
SUMMARY:Business as Unusual: The 2026 Shuford Lecture Featuring Michael Cro
 oke
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/business-as-unusual-the-2026-s
 huford-lecture-featuring-michael-crooke
CATEGORIES:Lectures
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073142Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53682646277287
DTSTART:20261113T000000Z
DTEND:20261113T010000Z
DESCRIPTION:Sankai Juku creates work that unfolds slowly and deliberately\,
  into what can feel like a new world\, where forces like time and gravity 
 seem to act on us in radically new ways.\n\n \n\nIn TOTEM: Void and Height
 \, dancers move with extraordinary presence and precision. Practicing the 
 Japanese dance tradition of Butoh\, the company’s performances exist som
 ehow between stillness and action\, between gravity and transcendence—an
 d sometimes feel less like watching dance and more like stepping inside a 
 waking dream. \n\n \n\nThe final work choreographed by their founder Ushio
  Amagatsu before his death in 2024\, TOTEM: Void and Height reflects a lif
 elong exploration of the relationship between the human body\, the natural
  world\, and the forces that shape them both. Through striking imagery and
  breathtaking physical control\, the performance invites audiences to slow
  down\, pay attention\, and experience time differently.\n\n \n\nThis is a
  rare opportunity to encounter one of the world’s most influential conte
 mporary dance companies and the final artistic vision of the artist who sh
 aped it for more than five decades.\n\n \n\nStudent tickets always $11. El
 igible for CLE. Find out more and buy tickets at: https://carolinaperformi
 ngarts.org/events/totem/
GEO:35.911208;-79.051989
LOCATION:Memorial Hall
SUMMARY:Sankai Juku TOTEM: Void and Height
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/sankai-juku-totem-void-and-hei
 ght
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073142Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54187615216720
DTSTART:20261113T130000Z
DTEND:20261113T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:Reception and artist conversation: October 29\, 2026\, 5:00 pm\
 n\n \n\nWeight of Light is an exhibition of seven works by multidisciplina
 ry artist taylor barnes\, created during her year-long residency at West O
 rchid in Pine Lake\, Georgia. Weight of Light considers the relationship b
 etween material\, perception\, and the ongoing process of becoming. Emergi
 ng from a period of experimentation and personal reflection\, the works oc
 cupy an ambiguous space between painting and object. Neither fixed represe
 ntations nor resolved compositions\, the works function as thresholds—si
 tes of transition where questions of origin\, transformation\, and self-po
 ssession remain open.\n\n \n\nbarnes approaches the canvas as a material s
 tructure rather than a passive surface. Through staining\, layering\, satu
 ration\, and the transformation of matter between solid and liquid states\
 , she explores the physical and conceptual possibilities of charcoal. Mate
 rials shift\, settle\, resist\, and respond\, creating conditions in which
  chance\, absence\, and emergence become integral to the work. The works d
 o not seek resolution through conventional notions of completion. Instead\
 , they retain evidence of their making—gestures\, interruptions\, accumu
 lations\, and erasures that reveal the instability and possibility inheren
 t in transformation. What remains visible is a record of decisions\, encou
 nters\, and material negotiations. The works resist linear narratives of p
 rogress\, suggesting instead a cyclical understanding of origin\, movement
 \, and revelation. Abstract maps\, they trace an unfolding relationship be
 tween the known and obscured. Darkness and light emerge not as opposing fo
 rces\, but as interdependent conditions which continually refigure percept
 ion and understanding. Black\, the central color and philosophical foundat
 ion of barnes' practice\, becomes a space of depth\, presence\, and possib
 ility—a means of considering how material holds identity\, memory\, and 
 lived experience. Informed by bell hooks' writings on objectification and 
 subjectivity\, barnes' work engages questions of agency\, visibility\, and
  the reclamation of self. Ultimately\, Weight of Light proposes ascension 
 not as transcendence or arrival\, but as an ongoing practice of self-posse
 ssion. Through material experimentation and an embrace of uncertainty\, ba
 rnes creates space for the possibility that becoming is neither linear nor
  complete\, but continually negotiated through acts of making\, unmaking\,
  and reclamation.\n\n \n\ntaylor barnes is a multidisciplinary artist work
 ing across ceramics\, fibers\, and drawing. Her practice investigates spir
 ituality\, oral history\, and structures of power through material and for
 m. Working primarily with clay\, fabric\, string\, and charcoal\, she tran
 slates emotion and lived experience into material forms that hold traces o
 f labor\, memory\, and ritual. barnes received her MFA in Fibers from the 
 University of North Texas. She lives and works between Georgia and Austin\
 , Texas.\n\n \n\nAdmission: Free\n\nGallery Hours: Monday-Friday\, 8 am-5 
 pm\n\n \n\nA weeknight or daytime permit is now required after 5:00 pm on 
 weekdays. No permit is required from 5:00 pm Friday through 7:30 am Monday
 . A $1.00 one-night pass is available in selected lots. More information c
 an be found HERE.\n\n \n\nArtist website: https://www.barnesartistry.com/ 
 \n\n \n\nFor more information\, please contact: Annette Lawrence\, annette
 l@unc.edu \n\n \n\nImage credit: Courtesy of the artist
GEO:35.912392;-79.054366
LOCATION:Hanes Art Center\, John and June Allcott Gallery
SUMMARY:John and June Allcott Gallery: taylor barnes\, Weight of Light
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/john-and-june-allcott-gallery-
 taylor-barnes-weight-of-light
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
