DESCRIPTION:For anyone who has ever questioned their place\, pushed against
  expectations\, or wondered what it costs to remain true to yourself\, The
  Surge: An Ode to Sinéad O’Connor is for you.\n\nInspired by the music\
 , words\, and enduring spirit of Sinéad O’Connor\, director and choreog
 rapher Sonya Tayeh creates a powerful dance experience performed by ten wo
 men over the age of 40. O’Connor’s music and narration\, drawn from he
 r memoir Rememberings\, weave throughout the performance. \n\n \n\nThe Sur
 ge is a reflection on identity\, aging\, freedom\, and the courage require
 d to live authentically in a world that often rewards conformity. What eme
 rges is both deeply personal and unexpectedly communal: a celebration of w
 omen who refuse to disappear\, soften themselves for others\, or surrender
  the right to define their own lives.\n\n \n\nStudent tickets always $11. 
 Eligible for CLE. Find out more and buy tickets at: https://carolinaperfor
 mingarts.org/events/the-surge/
GEO:35.911208;-79.051989
LOCATION:Memorial Hall
SUMMARY:The Surge: An Ode to Sinéad O’Connor
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-surge-an-ode-to-sinead-oco
 nnor
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073145Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54567507996836
DTSTART:20270312T140000Z
DTEND:20270312T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:Data generation is at the heart of high-quality\, impactful qua
 litative research. Without a solid empirical basis of sufficiently rich da
 ta\, producing a high-quality study is challenging. This course will discu
 ss rationales for choosing one or several data generation methods (individ
 ual interviews\, focus groups\, and field observations) that best align wi
 th project research questions. We will talk through developing data genera
 tion schedules (what to do when)\, addressing participant recruitment conc
 erns\, developing appropriately open questions\, strategies for follow-up 
 probing\, and building rapport with participants for both ethical and data
  quality reasons. The course will also address considerations regarding ge
 nerating data with individuals from vulnerable\, disadvantaged\, and margi
 nalized groups.\n\n \n\nMore specific topics will include the following:\n
 \n \n\nInitial considerations for high-quality data generation\, such as p
 articipant recruitmentAddressing sample size: “How many participants do 
 I need?”Sequencing of data generationConducting and reviewing pilot inte
 rviews“Requirements” of established methodologiesThe role of researche
 r positionalities and ethical issuesAddressing the focus group “effect
 ” and skills for facilitating a focus group“Being an ethnographer”: 
 Level of engagement in the field necessary for generating effective observ
 ationsConsidering what is to be “observed” and writing valuable field 
 notesPreparing for analysis during data generation \n\nThe course will inc
 lude multiple practical exercises to engage participants in developing and
  refining their knowledge and skills. \n\n \n\nRegistration and fee (refun
 dable for UNC-Chapel Hill students) are required for Zoom link access.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Qualitative Data Generation: Conducting Interviews\, Focus Groups\,
  and Field Observations
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/qualitative-data-generation-co
 nducting-interviews-focus-groups-and-field-observations
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20261128T073145Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54549796464861
DTSTART:20270318T180000Z
DTEND:20270318T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:In this advanced topics course\, we will present several common
  use cases for automations using the most updated and common workflows tha
 t align with the intended use cases for the Qualtrics survey tool.\n\nRegi
 stration and fee (refundable for UNC-Chapel Hill students upon attendance)
  are required for Zoom link access.
LOCATION:
